export const DATA_74: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-107", num: 107, title: "Đề 107", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Similarity, Difference, and the Making of Happy Partnerships", paragraphs: ["The question of whether romantic partners should be alike or different has fascinated psychologists, philosophers, and ordinary people in equal measure for centuries. Popular wisdom has long been divided on the matter, producing two competing proverbs that appear in the folklore of cultures around the world: \"birds of a feather flock together\" suggests that similarity is the foundation of lasting attraction, while \"opposites attract\" implies that difference and complementarity are what keep relationships alive and interesting. Both sayings contain a grain of truth, but neither captures the full complexity of what psychological research now tells us about the conditions that make romantic partnerships genuinely fulfilling over the long term.", "The strongest evidence from relationship research supports the role of similarity in the formation and early success of romantic partnerships. Studies consistently show that people are most likely to be initially attracted to others who share their values, educational background, political beliefs, and broad life goals. This tendency, known as assortative mating, is observed across cultures and is particularly strong for characteristics that individuals consider central to their identity. A person who places great importance on religious faith, for example, is likely to feel a strong and immediate connection with a partner who shares that commitment, while someone who values intellectual curiosity and academic achievement will typically find these qualities attractive in a potential partner.", "Shared values are especially important for long-term relationship satisfaction. Research conducted over several decades has found that couples who agree on fundamental questions — how to manage finances, how to raise children, how much social contact to seek, and what constitutes a meaningful life — report significantly higher levels of relationship quality and are less likely to experience serious conflict or separation. The mechanism behind this finding is straightforward: when partners share core values, they naturally make compatible decisions in the areas of life that matter most, reducing the frequency and intensity of disagreements and creating a stable foundation from which both individuals can pursue their goals.", "Yet difference between partners is not without its own value, and the relationship between similarity and happiness is more nuanced than a simple preference for sameness. Research in personality psychology has found that certain personality differences between partners can be genuinely complementary rather than conflicting. An introverted person, for example, may find that a more socially confident partner opens doors to new experiences and friendships that they would not have sought independently, while the extroverted partner may value the calm, reflective quality that the introvert brings to the relationship. What matters in such cases is not the presence of difference itself but whether partners recognize and appreciate the qualities in each other that they themselves lack.", "The distinction between surface-level differences and deep-level differences is also important. Partners who differ in superficial characteristics — tastes in food, preferences in music, hobbies and leisure activities — tend to find that these differences add variety and interest to the relationship rather than causing significant friction. Deep-level differences, by contrast — diverging views on the importance of family, conflicting ambitions, or fundamentally different approaches to trust and communication — are far more likely to produce lasting tension and dissatisfaction. Research suggests that happy couples are often those who have learned to celebrate surface differences while maintaining a bedrock of shared values and mutual respect at the deeper levels of the relationship.", "Perhaps the most important finding to emerge from decades of relationship research is that neither similarity nor difference is sufficient on its own to guarantee happiness. What seems to matter more than either variable is the quality of the communication and emotional attunement between partners — their ability to listen to each other with genuine curiosity, to express needs and feelings without contempt or defensiveness, and to repair the inevitable small ruptures that occur in any close relationship. A couple who are very similar may still become unhappy if they cannot communicate openly, while a couple with significant differences may thrive if they have developed the habits of mutual respect and honest dialogue that sustain closeness over time."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The proverb \"opposites attract\" has been proven false by psychological research, which consistently favors similarity as the basis for happiness.", "Surface-level differences in hobbies and tastes are the most reliable predictor of long-term relationship satisfaction and stability.", "Happy partnerships depend on a combination of shared core values, complementary differences, and above all, the quality of communication between partners.", "Research conclusively proves that similar partners are always happier than those with significant differences in personality and values."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what term describes the tendency to be attracted to people who share similar values and backgrounds?", options: ["Selective affinity, a term used to describe the brain's preference for familiar stimuli when evaluating potential romantic partners.", "Assortative mating, the pattern in which people are most attracted to others who share their core characteristics and identity.", "Value convergence, a phenomenon in which partners gradually adopt each other's beliefs over the course of a relationship.", "Complementary bonding, the process by which individuals seek partners whose strengths compensate for their own weaknesses."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, agreement on which of the following topics is most strongly linked to long-term relationship quality?", options: ["Approaches to fitness, dietary habits, and the management of personal health and medical decisions.", "Fundamental questions such as managing finances, raising children, and what constitutes a meaningful life.", "Opinions about current affairs, cultural tastes, and the allocation of household responsibilities and duties.", "Preferences in interior design, holiday destinations, and the choice of social events to attend as a couple."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how might an introverted person benefit from having an extroverted partner?", options: ["The extroverted partner can manage all social obligations on behalf of both people, allowing the introvert to avoid uncomfortable situations.", "The extroverted partner may open the introvert to new experiences and friendships they would not have sought on their own.", "The extroverted partner provides constant encouragement that gradually transforms the introvert into a more sociable person.", "The extroverted partner attracts a wider social circle whose energy and enthusiasm positively affect the introvert's mental health."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"fulfilling\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Intellectually stimulating and centered on a shared passion for learning, culture, and personal development.", "Deeply satisfying in a way that meets emotional, personal, and relational needs over a sustained period of time.", "Commercially successful and widely recognized as a model relationship by friends, family, and the surrounding community.", "Practically convenient and free of the logistical complications that frequently arise in long-distance relationships."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"these\" in paragraph 2 (\"will typically find these qualities attractive in a potential partner\") refers to _______.", options: ["Religious commitment and a sense of shared spiritual purpose, which are identified as central to strong initial attraction.", "Political beliefs and educational background, which the earlier part of the paragraph identifies as key drivers of initial compatibility.", "Core identity characteristics in general, which the passage describes as the foundation of assortative mating across cultures.", "Intellectual curiosity and academic achievement, which are mentioned as qualities a person who values them will seek in a partner."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"appreciate\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["To adopt a quality from one's partner by observing and imitating their behavior over time.", "To compensate for something by developing equivalent strengths in other areas of personality.", "To recognize and value something genuinely, understanding the positive contribution it makes.", "To tolerate something with patience, accepting it as an unavoidable feature of a long-term relationship."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which type of difference between partners is most likely to cause lasting tension in a relationship?", options: ["Different tastes in leisure activities that result in partners spending increasing amounts of time pursuing separate hobbies.", "Different musical preferences that create an atmosphere of persistent low-level irritation during shared time at home.", "Deep-level differences such as diverging views on family, conflicting ambitions, or different approaches to trust and communication.", "Different preferences in food and cuisine that lead to recurring disagreements over meal choices and restaurant visits."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a surface-level difference that adds variety rather than causing conflict?", options: ["Differing preferences in food and cuisine.", "Diverging views on the importance of family and long-term commitments.", "Different tastes in music and entertainment.", "Different hobbies and leisure activity preferences."], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the final paragraph about the most important factor in a happy relationship?", options: ["Couples who are highly similar in personality and values will always be happier than couples who differ significantly from each other.", "The ability to communicate openly, listen with curiosity, and repair conflicts matters more than whether partners are similar or different.", "Similarity in core values is the single most reliable predictor of relationship happiness and should be prioritized above all else.", "Partners who differ greatly in personality should seek professional counseling early in their relationship to prevent future conflicts."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Left-Handedness: From Stigma to Recognition", paragraphs: ["In a world built overwhelmingly for right-handed people, being left-handed has historically meant navigating a landscape of daily inconveniences, social suspicion, and, in many cultures, outright discrimination. For most of recorded human history, left-handedness was viewed not as a neutral biological trait but as a sign of moral deviance, intellectual inferiority, or supernatural influence. The very language that encodes this bias persists today: the Latin word for left, sinister , evolved into an English adjective meaning threatening or evil, while the French word gauche, originally meaning left-sided, came to denote social awkwardness and poor taste. These etymological traces reveal how thoroughly the stigmatization of left-handedness was woven into Western cultural consciousness.", "Estimates of the prevalence of left-handedness in the human population have remained remarkably stable across studies conducted in different countries and historical periods, consistently placing the proportion of left-handers at approximately ten percent of the global population. This consistency suggests that left-handedness is largely determined by genetic and neurological factors rather than cultural ones, though the mechanisms of inheritance are complex and not yet fully understood. What is clear is that left-handedness is associated with a different pattern of cerebral lateralization — the division of cognitive functions between the left and right hemispheres of the brain — in which language processing and fine motor control are distributed differently than in the majority of right-handed individuals.", "Despite comprising only a small minority of the population, left-handers have achieved a remarkable level of representation among historically celebrated creative and intellectual figures. Leonardo da Vinci, whose notebooks are filled with his characteristic mirror-writing, is perhaps the most famous left-hander in history; Michelangelo, Raphael, and many other Renaissance artists are also reported to have favored the left hand. Among political leaders, Alexander the Great, Julius Caesar, Napoleon Bonaparte, and in more recent history Barack Obama and Bill Clinton were all left-handed. The list of left-handed musicians, writers, and scientists is equally distinguished, leading some researchers to speculate about a possible link between left-handedness and exceptional creative or analytical ability — though the evidence for any such connection remains contested and inconclusive.", "The practical challenges faced by left-handers in a right-handed world are more mundane but no less real. For much of history, everyday tools and instruments — scissors, knives, writing implements, musical instruments, and industrial machinery — were designed exclusively for right-handed use, forcing left-handers to adapt or struggle. In school settings across many countries and cultures, children who showed a natural preference for the left hand were frequently forced to write with their right, a practice that persisted in parts of Europe, Asia, and the Americas well into the second half of the twentieth century and that research has since linked to increased rates of anxiety, stammering, and reading difficulties in affected individuals.", "The twentieth century brought gradual but significant improvements in the social and material circumstances of left-handers. The practice of forcing left-handed children to switch was abandoned in most Western educational systems by the 1970s and 1980s, following growing awareness of the psychological harm it caused. More tangibly, the market for left-handed products began to expand substantially from the 1980s onward. Today, a left-handed person can purchase scissors, can openers, computer mice, guitars, notebooks with spiral bindings on the right side, ink pens designed to prevent smudging, and a wide range of kitchen utensils all specifically designed for left-hand use — a development that would have been inconceivable to earlier generations. Specialist retailers and online stores dedicated entirely to left-handed products now serve what has become a recognized and commercially viable consumer segment.", "The scientific interest in left-handedness has also grown considerably in recent decades. Researchers in neuroscience, genetics, and cognitive psychology have investigated the neurological basis of handedness with increasing sophistication, producing findings that challenge earlier assumptions about the cognitive profile of left-handers. While popular stereotypes have sometimes portrayed left-handers as either especially gifted or especially prone to certain difficulties, the research paints a more complex picture: left-handers show greater individual variation in brain organization than right-handers, and this variation is associated with a wider range of outcomes rather than a consistent advantage or disadvantage in any particular domain.", "International Left-Handers Day, celebrated on August 13 each year since its establishment in 1992, reflects the degree to which left-handedness has been rehabilitated as a cultural identity rather than merely a physical characteristic. What was once a source of shame and enforced correction is now, for many left-handers, a point of pride — a marker of membership in a minority that includes some of history's most celebrated minds, and a reminder that the dominant hand one happens to use says nothing meaningful about one's character, intelligence, or worth."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Scientific research has definitively proven that left-handedness is caused by a genetic mutation affecting cerebral lateralization.", "Left-handedness has evolved from a stigmatized and poorly understood trait into a recognized aspect of human diversity that is increasingly accommodated.", "Left-handed people are statistically more likely than right-handers to achieve greatness in creative and intellectual fields.", "The primary challenge facing left-handers today is the lack of affordable specialist products designed for their specific needs."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, approximately what proportion of the global population is left-handed?", options: ["Around five percent, a figure that has declined slightly as forced right-hand training became common in many school systems.", "Roughly fifteen percent, a proportion that varies significantly between countries depending on cultural attitudes toward left-handedness.", "Approximately ten percent, a figure that has remained consistent across studies in different countries and time periods.", "About twenty percent, a proportion that neuroscientists believe reflects the natural distribution of brain lateralization patterns."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following individuals is specifically described as using mirror-writing in their notebooks?", options: ["Michelangelo, whose unconventional working methods extended to writing his personal correspondence in reversed script.", "Leonardo da Vinci, whose notebooks are filled with his characteristic mirror-writing and who is described as the most famous left-hander in history.", "Alexander the Great, whose left-handedness is cited as evidence of the connection between handedness and strategic genius.", "Napoleon Bonaparte, who reportedly developed mirror-writing as a private code to protect his military correspondence."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which negative outcomes have been linked to forcing left-handed children to write with their right hand?", options: ["Increased rates of anxiety, stammering, and reading difficulties in the individuals who were subjected to this practice.", "Reduced academic attainment, poor social adjustment, and a lifelong preference for solitary rather than group learning activities.", "Lower scores in creative subjects such as art and music, combined with heightened aggression in social settings.", "Permanent changes to brain lateralization that impair the development of both fine motor skills and spatial reasoning."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"sinister\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Socially awkward and graceless, lacking the polish and refinement expected in formal situations.", "Rare and unusual, belonging to a small group set apart from the majority of the population.", "Unfamiliar and difficult to understand, associated with beliefs and customs that fall outside mainstream cultural norms.", "Threatening or suggesting evil, carrying a sense of danger or malevolent intention."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 5 (\"growing awareness of the psychological harm it caused\") refers to _______.", options: ["The broader social stigma attached to left-handedness, which educators in the twentieth century sought to eliminate.", "The expansion of the left-handed product market, which researchers argued created unrealistic expectations among young left-handers.", "The practice of forcing left-handed children to switch to writing with their right hand.", "Left-handedness itself, whose neurological differences were once incorrectly believed to cause psychological difficulties."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"achieved\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Formally awarded by institutions that historically recognized left-handedness as a marker of exceptional intellectual potential.", "Accidentally acquired through a combination of fortunate circumstances and the absence of competitive right-handed rivals.", "Attained through effort and ability, reaching a level of distinction that is recognized and celebrated by others.", "Inherited from a lineage of similarly talented individuals, suggesting a genetic basis for creative and intellectual greatness."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following left-handed products is specifically described as designed to prevent a practical writing difficulty?", options: ["Scissors with handles shaped to accommodate the grip and cutting angle of the left hand.", "Spiral-bound notebooks with the binding placed on the right side rather than the conventional left.", "Computer mice ergonomically shaped to fit comfortably in the left hand during extended use.", "Ink pens specifically designed to prevent smudging as the left hand moves across freshly written text."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a left-handed product available from specialist retailers?", options: ["Guitars adapted for left-handed players.", "Scissors designed for left-hand use.", "Wristwatches with the winding crown on the left side for easier left-handed operation.", "Can openers designed to be operated with the left hand."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 6 about the author's view of the claim that left-handers are especially gifted?", options: ["The author suggests that the claim is probably false but has been preserved in popular culture because it flatters left-handed readers.", "The author fully endorses the claim, arguing that the list of famous left-handers is strong enough evidence to confirm a causal link.", "The author is entirely neutral, presenting both supporting and contradicting evidence without offering any evaluative judgment.", "The author treats the claim skeptically, noting that research shows greater variation rather than consistent advantage in left-handers."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Opening Doors: The Era of Economic Liberalization in Historical Perspective", paragraphs: ["Among the most consequential transformations in modern economic history, the wave of deliberate market-opening policies that swept through developing and transitional economies during the final decades of the twentieth century stands out for both its geographic breadth and its long-term structural impact. From China's landmark reforms under Deng Xiaoping beginning in 1978, to the collapse of centrally planned economies in Eastern Europe and the former Soviet Union in the late 1980s and early 1990s, to the wave of trade liberalization that accompanied the debt crisis responses of Latin American nations, the global turn toward economic openness reshaped the productive capacity, trade relationships, and social fabric of dozens of nations within a relatively compressed historical window. [1] Understanding the conditions that gave rise to this shift, the mechanisms through which it was implemented, and the uneven outcomes it produced remains one of the central preoccupations of economic historians and development scholars.", "The intellectual foundations of economic liberalization were laid over several decades by economists who challenged the dominant post-war consensus in favor of state-led industrialization and import substitution. By the 1970s, economists associated with the so-called Washington Consensus had developed a coherent policy framework emphasizing macroeconomic stabilization, the removal of trade barriers, privatization of state-owned enterprises, deregulation of domestic markets, and the opening of capital accounts to international investment. This framework was promoted by influential institutions including the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank, which increasingly made access to emergency financing conditional on the adoption of structural adjustment programs aligned with these principles. For governments facing acute fiscal crises and debt burdens, these conditionality arrangements often left little genuine choice about the direction of reform.", "China's economic opening represented a distinctive model that differed in important respects from the shock-therapy approaches applied in some Eastern European economies. Rather than simultaneous and comprehensive liberalization, Deng Xiaoping's leadership pursued a strategy of gradual, sequenced reform that maintained Communist Party control over the political system while selectively introducing market incentives at the margins of the planned economy. Special Economic Zones, established in coastal provinces beginning in 1980, served as controlled laboratories for market mechanisms — attracting foreign direct investment, experimenting with export-oriented manufacturing, and generating demonstration effects that informed the expansion of market principles to wider sectors of the economy. [2] This dual-track approach allowed China to capture the productivity gains of market competition without exposing the entire economy to the instability that rapid liberalization produced elsewhere.", "The experience of Eastern Europe and the former Soviet Union provided a starkly contrasting case study. Following the political upheavals of 1989 and 1991, many of these economies pursued rapid and comprehensive market transitions, abolishing price controls, privatizing state enterprises, and opening their borders to foreign trade and investment within a matter of years. The immediate consequences in several countries were severe: industrial output collapsed, unemployment rose sharply, and the withdrawal of state subsidies exposed millions of households to poverty levels that had been largely concealed under the planned economy. Poland and the Czech Republic eventually stabilized and achieved sustained growth, but other economies — most notably Russia — experienced a prolonged period of economic contraction and institutional dysfunction that lasted well into the 1990s.", "Latin America's encounter with economic liberalization was shaped primarily by the debt crisis that erupted in 1982, when Mexico announced it could no longer service its external debt obligations, triggering a regional financial crisis that forced governments across the continent to seek emergency assistance from international creditors. The structural adjustment packages that followed typically required reductions in public expenditure, the removal of import tariffs and export subsidies, exchange rate devaluation, and the privatization of state-owned utilities and industrial enterprises. [3] While these reforms succeeded in restoring macroeconomic stability and attracting renewed foreign investment in many cases, their social costs were substantial: inequality increased in most reforming countries, and the benefits of growth were distributed unevenly, contributing to political backlashes that would reshape the region's political landscape in subsequent decades.", "The legacy of the era of economic liberalization is deeply contested. Proponents point to the dramatic reductions in absolute poverty achieved in East Asia, particularly in China, where hundreds of millions of people were lifted out of poverty in the decades following the 1978 reforms — a development that has no parallel in human history in terms of its speed and scale. Critics counter that this success was achieved through a model that bore little resemblance to orthodox liberalization prescriptions, and that the more faithfully orthodox reforms applied in Sub-Saharan Africa and parts of Latin America produced far more mixed results. [4] The scholarly debate about the conditions under which economic openness generates broadly shared prosperity, rather than concentrated gains and diffuse social costs, remains unresolved and continues to inform contemporary policy discussions about trade, investment, and the appropriate role of the state in economic development.", "What the historical record suggests with reasonable confidence is that economic liberalization is not a monolithic policy but a heterogeneous set of practices whose outcomes depend critically on the sequencing of reforms, the institutional capacity of the reforming state, the external economic environment, and the political coalitions capable of sustaining reform over time. No single episode — neither China's extraordinary success nor Russia's turbulent transition — should be taken as a universal template. The challenge for economists and policymakers today is to extract transferable lessons from this complex historical experience without flattening its irreducible complexity into simplistic prescriptions."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main argument of the passage?", options: ["Economic liberalization is always preferable to state-led development, as demonstrated by the failures of centrally planned economies in Eastern Europe.", "China's economic reforms under Deng Xiaoping represent the only genuinely successful model of economic liberalization and should be universally adopted.", "The Washington Consensus was fundamentally flawed from its inception and caused more harm than good in every country where it was applied.", "The era of economic liberalization was a global phenomenon with varied and complex outcomes that resist simple evaluation as success or failure."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following was part of the policy framework associated with the Washington Consensus?", options: ["Removing trade barriers, privatizing state enterprises, and opening capital accounts to international investment.", "Establishing special economic zones as controlled experiments before extending market reforms more broadly.", "Prioritizing income redistribution and social welfare spending before pursuing macroeconomic stabilization.", "Expanding state ownership of key industries to protect them from the volatility of international capital markets."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what role did China's Special Economic Zones play in the reform process?", options: ["They were established in inland provinces to reduce inequality between China's coastal and interior regions.", "They were used to isolate foreign companies from the domestic economy and prevent market principles from spreading too quickly.", "They served as controlled laboratories for market mechanisms, attracting investment and generating lessons for broader reform.", "They replaced state-owned enterprises entirely, signaling China's full commitment to rapid and comprehensive liberalization."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which two Eastern European economies are specifically mentioned as having eventually achieved sustained growth?", options: ["Poland and the Czech Republic, which stabilized and achieved sustained growth despite early difficulties during the transition.", "Ukraine and Belarus, which maintained closer ties with Russia and therefore experienced a less severe initial economic shock.", "Bulgaria and Slovakia, which benefited from early EU membership that provided structural funds and market access.", "Hungary and Romania, which pursued gradual reforms that avoided the sharp industrial decline experienced by their neighbors."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"adoption\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["The formal acceptance and implementation of a set of policies as required conditions for receiving financial assistance.", "The voluntary selection of best practices from multiple international models following independent government review.", "The temporary suspension of existing economic regulations pending the completion of a negotiated reform timetable.", "The gradual modification of a policy framework to suit the specific institutional conditions of each borrowing country."], answer: 0 }, { q: "In paragraph 3 (\"the instability that rapid liberalization produced elsewhere \") — which concept does the phrase \"rapid liberalization\" contrast with in this paragraph?", options: ["China's dual-track approach of gradual, sequenced reform that maintained political control while selectively introducing market incentives.", "The Washington Consensus framework, which the passage describes as a rigid set of prescriptions applied uniformly across all reforming economies.", "The shock-therapy strategy used in Latin America, which prioritized debt repayment over the gradual introduction of market mechanisms.", "The Special Economic Zones policy, which was designed to limit the geographic spread of market reforms to coastal areas."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what event triggered the Latin American debt crisis that led to structural adjustment programs across the region?", options: ["The withdrawal of IMF emergency lending facilities from the region following concerns about misuse of funds in several countries.", "Mexico's announcement in 1982 that it could no longer service its external debt, triggering a regional financial crisis.", "A coordinated decision by Latin American governments to default simultaneously in order to gain negotiating leverage with creditors.", "A sharp fall in global commodity prices in 1982 that caused simultaneous export revenue collapses across multiple Latin American nations."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"contraction\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["A temporary slowdown in growth caused by external shocks that resolved once global commodity prices recovered.", "A sustained period of economic shrinkage and decline in output, employment, and living standards.", "A deliberate policy of reducing industrial capacity to improve efficiency and competitiveness in international export markets.", "A structural shift away from heavy industry toward a service-based economy driven by private sector investment."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a requirement of structural adjustment packages in Latin America?", options: ["Removal of import tariffs and export subsidies to open domestic markets.", "Privatization of state-owned utilities and industrial enterprises.", "Reductions in public expenditure and government spending programs.", "Mandatory redistribution of privatized enterprise shares to low-income households."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Yet the pace, sequencing, and political context of these transitions varied enormously, producing a spectrum of outcomes that defies any single explanatory framework.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Art and Economics of Hotel Room Service", paragraphs: ["Among the amenities that distinguish a full-service hotel from simpler lodging options, room service occupies a uniquely ambivalent position. On one hand, it represents the pinnacle of hospitality — the promise that a guest, without leaving the privacy and comfort of their room, can have almost any food or beverage delivered to their door at virtually any hour. On the other hand, room service is operationally complex, financially precarious, and frequently cited by hotel executives as one of the least profitable components of the food and beverage operation. Understanding why room service persists, how it is organized, and what forces are reshaping it in the contemporary hospitality industry requires engaging with both the cultural expectations that surround luxury accommodation and the hard economic realities of running a kitchen around the clock.", "The mechanics of delivering food and beverages to a guest room are considerably more involved than the seamless experience that guests typically perceive. An order placed by a guest triggers a sequence of coordinated activities: the order is received by a room service operator, relayed to the relevant kitchen stations, prepared according to the specific requirements of the dish, plated and assembled on a tray or trolley with the appropriate condiments, cutlery, glassware, and accompaniments, covered to retain temperature during transit, and then transported by a service attendant — often via elevator through multiple floors — to the guest's room. [1] The entire process, from the moment of ordering to delivery at the door, is typically expected to take no longer than thirty to forty-five minutes, a standard that demands precise coordination between the front-of-house team and the kitchen.", "The range of items available through room service menus varies considerably across hotel categories. Budget and mid-scale hotels may offer a limited selection of cold sandwiches, beverages, and packaged snacks that require minimal preparation and can be assembled quickly without significant kitchen staffing. Full-service and luxury hotels, by contrast, typically maintain a room service menu that mirrors or closely approximates the offerings of their in-house restaurant, including hot dishes, multi-course meals, specialty beverages, and in many properties, a twenty-four-hour breakfast option. The preparation of these items requires a dedicated room service kitchen station — or at minimum, a designated team on each kitchen shift capable of managing room service orders alongside the primary restaurant service without compromising quality or speed.", "Temperature management is one of the most technically demanding aspects of food delivery to hotel rooms. Hot dishes begin to lose heat the moment they leave the kitchen, and the transit time between preparation and consumption — which may include elevator waits, corridor navigation, and the time for the attendant to set up the tray in the room — is sufficient to significantly affect the temperature of sensitive items such as soups, grilled proteins, and delicate sauces. Hotels have invested in a range of technological solutions to address this challenge, including insulated food delivery bags, heated trolleys with built-in temperature maintenance systems, and cloche covers that trap heat around individual dishes. [2] Even with these measures , kitchen teams are trained to account for transit time when timing the completion of dishes, aiming to have items arrive at the room at service temperature rather than at the temperature they left the kitchen.", "The financial economics of room service have become increasingly challenging in the contemporary hotel industry. Labor costs represent the largest single expense: a dedicated room service operation requires staffing for order-taking, food preparation, tray assembly, delivery, and the collection and return of used equipment — functions that must be maintained across extended service windows that often include late night and early morning hours when demand is low but minimum staffing levels cannot be reduced below a certain threshold. These fixed costs must be recovered through a relatively small volume of orders, which is why room service pricing typically carries a significant premium over equivalent menu items in the hotel restaurant, supplemented in most properties by a dedicated room service charge and, in many cultures, an expected gratuity for the delivery attendant. [3]", "The advent of third-party food delivery platforms has introduced a new competitive dynamic into the room service landscape. Guests who might once have accepted the convenience premium of in-house room service now have the option of ordering from a wide range of local restaurants through apps such as Uber Eats, DoorDash, or their regional equivalents, often at a fraction of the cost. This development has prompted many hotel operators to reconsider the scope and model of their room service offerings. Some properties have responded by partnering with local restaurants to expand the range of cuisine available through their own ordering systems; others have moved toward a hybrid model in which in-house production is limited to a reduced core menu while external delivery options are actively facilitated and communicated to guests. [4]", "Despite these commercial pressures, room service retains a symbolic importance that is difficult to quantify but impossible to dismiss. For guests celebrating special occasions, recovering from illness, managing young children, or simply seeking the restorative pleasure of a meal enjoyed in solitude or intimacy, the ability to have thoughtfully prepared food delivered with discretion and care to their private space remains an expression of hospitality at its most personal. Hotel operators who have eliminated room service entirely in favor of self-service pantries or lobby grab-and-go stations have frequently discovered that the absence of this amenity registers disproportionately in guest satisfaction surveys, suggesting that what room service delivers is not merely food but a form of emotional reassurance that is central to the meaning of luxury accommodation."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central argument of the passage?", options: ["Room service is operationally complex and financially challenging, yet retains an irreplaceable symbolic value within the luxury hospitality experience.", "Room service should be eliminated by hotels facing financial pressure, as third-party delivery apps provide a more cost-effective alternative for guests.", "The primary challenge facing room service today is temperature management, which technology has not yet solved to guests' satisfaction.", "Hotels that invest in upgrading their room service infrastructure will always achieve higher guest satisfaction scores than those that do not."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the standard time expectation for delivering a room service order from the moment of placing to arrival at the door?", options: ["Between thirty and forty-five minutes, a window that demands precise coordination between the front-of-house team and the kitchen.", "Up to one hour for hot meals, though cold items and beverages are typically delivered within twenty minutes of ordering.", "No longer than fifteen to twenty minutes, a standard maintained by using pre-prepared dishes that require only reheating.", "Exactly sixty minutes, as required by luxury hotel brand standards to ensure consistent guest experience across all properties."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which technological solution is specifically described as trapping heat around individual dishes?", options: ["Heated trolleys with built-in temperature maintenance systems that keep multiple dishes warm simultaneously during transit.", "Insulated food delivery bags that surround the entire tray and maintain an even temperature throughout the delivery journey.", "Cloche covers that trap heat around individual dishes, preventing temperature loss between the kitchen and the guest's room.", "Microwave stations installed on each floor of the hotel that allow attendants to reheat dishes immediately before delivery."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"profitable\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Popular with guests and highly rated in customer satisfaction surveys conducted by independent review platforms.", "Operationally straightforward and requiring minimal coordination between kitchen and front-of-house service teams.", "Strategically important for a hotel's brand reputation even when it does not generate direct financial returns.", "Capable of generating financial gains that exceed the costs involved in providing the service."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why does room service pricing typically carry a significant premium over equivalent restaurant menu items?", options: ["Because the high fixed labor costs of maintaining staff across extended service hours must be recovered from a small order volume.", "Because room service requires dedicated kitchen equipment that is separate from the main restaurant operation and must be financed separately.", "Because luxury hotels use higher-quality ingredients in room service dishes than in their main restaurant to justify the exclusivity.", "Because guests who order room service are willing to pay more for the convenience and are therefore charged at a higher rate."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The phrase \"these measures\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["The technological solutions for temperature management, including insulated bags, heated trolleys, and cloche covers.", "The coordination systems used to relay orders between the front-of-house operator and the relevant kitchen stations.", "The standard thirty-to-forty-five-minute delivery windows that hotels use as performance benchmarks for their room service teams.", "The training protocols used to teach kitchen teams how to time the completion of dishes to account for transit delays."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"maintain\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["To occasionally update something by adding new items in response to guest feedback and seasonal ingredient availability.", "To formally document and archive something so that it can be reproduced accurately across multiple hotel locations.", "To reduce gradually over time in response to declining demand while preserving the most popular items for occasional guests.", "To keep something in continuous existence at a consistent standard, ensuring it remains available without interruption."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how have some hotels responded to competition from third-party food delivery apps?", options: ["They have partnered with local restaurants to expand cuisine options or adopted hybrid models combining in-house and external delivery.", "They have invested in developing their own proprietary delivery apps to compete directly with established platforms in their markets.", "They have banned third-party delivery services from entering hotel premises to protect the revenue of their in-house food operations.", "They have significantly reduced room service prices to match the lower cost point offered by app-based food delivery services."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a situation in which guests particularly value room service?", options: ["When celebrating a special occasion such as an anniversary or birthday.", "When recovering from illness and unable or unwilling to visit the restaurant.", "When managing young children who are asleep and cannot be taken to the hotel restaurant.", "When traveling on a corporate expense account and seeking to maximize the quality of their dining experience."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'At the point of delivery, the attendant is expected to set up the tray or trolley within the room, present items professionally, and offer to pour beverages or explain the contents of each dish.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-168", num: 168, title: "Đề 168", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Old and New: How Japanese Weddings Blend Two Traditions", paragraphs: ["When many people imagine a Japanese wedding, they picture a couple in traditional dress standing quietly in front of a shrine. That image is still true for some couples today, but it is only part of the picture, since most modern weddings in Japan actually combine older customs with newer, Western-style elements, and the exact mix often depends on the couple's own families and personal taste.", "A traditional wedding usually takes place at a Shinto shrine and includes only a small number of guests, often just close family members. The bride wears a heavy white kimono called a shiromuku, and a Shinto priest leads the couple through a quiet , formal ceremony meant to introduce the new couple to the gods watched over by the shrine. Even the walk from the shrine gate to the main hall follows a set pattern, with the priest leading the way and the couple following a few steps behind, so that every part of the morning feels planned rather than casual.", "One of the most important moments in this ceremony is a ritual called san-san-kudo. The bride, the groom, and members of both families take turns sipping rice wine from three flat cups, drinking three small sips from each cup in a fixed order. The number three is considered lucky, and the shared sips are meant to represent the joining of two families into one. Guests who are not part of the immediate family usually cannot see this part of the ceremony at all, since it takes place inside the shrine's main hall rather than in a space open to the public.", "After the shrine ceremony, though, many couples move on to a large reception at a hotel or banquet hall, and this part of the day often looks very different. The bride may change out of her kimono into a white Western-style wedding dress, and later into a third outfit for photographs. Guests, for their part, are expected to bring cash gifts in special decorated envelopes, and there are clear, unwritten rules about how much money is considered appropriate to give, with the amount usually depending on how close the guest is to the couple and whether the guest is attending alone or with a partner, and giving too little or too much money is generally considered impolite by older relatives.", "For many couples today, mixing these two styles is not seen as strange at all. Instead, it lets them honor family tradition through the quiet shrine ceremony while still enjoying a large celebration with friends afterward, and wedding planners say this blended approach has become the most common choice among couples getting married in Japan now, even though the cost of holding two very different parts of the day in one weekend can be considerably higher than choosing just one style alone, a trade-off most couples say they are happy to accept."], questions: [{ q: "According to the passage, who typically attends a traditional Shinto shrine wedding ceremony?", options: ["Local community members who live near the shrine.", "Only the bride and groom, with no other guests present.", "Only a small number of guests, usually close family members.", "A large group of friends and coworkers invited to the reception."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"quiet\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["private and hidden from all guests", "calm and without loud noise", "slow-moving and long-lasting", "completely silent with no talking at all"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The bride wears a white kimono called a shiromuku during the shrine ceremony.", "Western-style wedding elements are now more important to Japanese couples than any traditional shrine customs.", "Nearly all Japanese couples today skip the traditional shrine ceremony completely.", "Modern Japanese weddings usually blend traditional and Western elements."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as part of a traditional Shinto wedding ceremony?", options: ["The ceremony is followed immediately by a fireworks display.", "The bride wears a heavy white kimono called a shiromuku.", "It takes place at a Shinto shrine.", "The couple and families share sips of rice wine from three cups."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["mixing traditional shrine ceremonies with Western-style receptions", "san-san-kudo ritual performed during the ceremony", "the cash gifts guests bring in decorated envelopes", "the shiromuku kimono worn during the shrine ceremony"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what happens during the san-san-kudo ritual?", options: ["The couple and families sip rice wine from three cups.", "Guests place cash gifts into decorated envelopes.", "The bride changes into a white Western-style dress.", "The couple exchanges rings in front of the shrine priest."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred about how Japanese couples today generally view combining traditional and Western wedding elements?", options: ["The bride typically wears a heavy white kimono called a shiromuku during the shrine portion of the ceremony.", "Wedding planners created the blended style in order to convince couples to abandon the shrine ceremony entirely.", "Older Japanese couples strongly disapprove of combining the two wedding styles.", "Most couples see it as a normal, accepted way to celebrate rather than something unusual."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"blended\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["performed smoothly without any mistakes", "combined together into a single approach", "completely replaced by something entirely new", "carefully planned many months in advance"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's purpose in describing both the shrine ceremony and the reception in the passage?", options: ["To illustrate how modern Japanese weddings integrate two distinct traditions.", "To argue that traditional shrine ceremonies are disappearing entirely in Japan.", "To explain the religious meaning behind every Shinto wedding ritual in detail.", "To criticize couples who choose a Western-style reception over a traditional one."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what are guests expected to do at the reception?", options: ["Give customary cash gifts in decorated envelopes.", "Help the bride change outfits between the ceremony and reception.", "Wear formal kimono matching the bride's outfit.", "Give a short speech about the couple during the ceremony."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Vietnam's Rapidly Shifting AI Landscape", paragraphs: ["Vietnam's engagement with artificial intelligence has shifted rapidly over the past several years, moving from a handful of university research groups experimenting with early machine-learning models to a landscape that now includes government strategy documents, dedicated funding programs, and a growing number of homegrown startups building AI products for both domestic and regional markets. Where discussions of the field were once confined largely to a small circle of specialists, they now regularly appear in mainstream business and policy conversations as well.", "Central to this shift has been a national strategy that sets targets for AI adoption across sectors such as healthcare, agriculture, and manufacturing by the end of the decade, alongside plans to expand the pool of specialists trained in the field; whether these targets are met, however, will depend heavily on how quickly supporting infrastructure — reliable data systems, computing capacity, and clear regulatory guidance — can be built out to match the ambition of the strategy itself. Officials involved in drafting the plan have acknowledged as much publicly, framing the targets less as guarantees than as a direction the country intends to move toward as quickly as circumstances allow.", "Universities have responded by expanding AI-related coursework and, in some cases, launching entirely new degree programs, though several administrators note that demand for qualified instructors has outpaced the supply of graduates capable of teaching at that level, creating a bottleneck that funding alone cannot immediately resolve, since training a new generation of instructors typically takes years longer than simply approving a budget for additional classrooms or equipment.", "Foreign interest has grown alongside domestic efforts. International chipmakers and technology firms have signed cooperation agreements with Vietnamese partners covering everything from workforce training to the construction of data centers, and several multinational companies have opened smaller research units in the country, drawn partly by comparatively lower operating costs and a young, technically inclined workforce. A handful of these arrangements also include commitments to train local engineers directly, though it remains unclear how many of the resulting jobs will involve substantive research work rather than more routine technical support.", "Domestic startups, meanwhile, have begun attracting notable rounds of venture funding, particularly in areas such as language processing for Vietnamese — a market underserved by large international AI models — and software tools aimed at small and medium-sized businesses that cannot afford custom-built systems. Investors in these rounds often point to Vietnam's large, digitally active population as a reason for optimism, even though several of the companies involved remain small by regional standards.", "Not every observer views this trajectory with equal optimism. Some industry analysts caution that much of the country's current activity remains concentrated in application-layer products built on top of foreign-developed models, rather than in the more fundamental research needed to produce genuinely original systems, and argue that without deeper investment in that foundational research, Vietnam risks remaining a consumer rather than a producer of core AI technology. Supporters of the current approach respond that building strong application-layer products is itself a reasonable first stage of development, and that foundational research becomes more realistic only once a country has accumulated enough capital, talent, and institutional experience to sustain it.", "Whether the country closes that gap will likely depend on the same factors already shaping its progress so far: how effectively the education pipeline can be scaled, whether regulatory frameworks keep pace with rapid deployment, and whether early venture-funded successes are followed by sustained investment rather than short-term enthusiasm. None of these questions is likely to be resolved quickly, and most analysts following the sector suggest that the coming several years, rather than any single policy announcement, will ultimately determine which direction the country's AI ambitions actually take."], questions: [{ q: "According to the passage, what challenge do universities face in expanding AI education?", options: ["A shortage of qualified instructors able to teach at the needed level.", "Low student interest in enrolling in AI-related coursework.", "Insufficient funding allocated by the government for new degree programs.", "A lack of reliable data systems needed to support AI research."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"bottleneck\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a complete and permanent stop to all progress", "a point that restricts or slows overall progress", "a type of financial investment fund", "a narrow physical container for liquids"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Foreign investment matters far less to Vietnam's AI progress than domestic startup funding does.", "Vietnamese universities have launched new AI degree programs to meet rising demand for coursework.", "Vietnam's AI sector grows quickly, yet its long-term future hinges on fixing key structural gaps.", "Vietnam has already become a global leader in producing original AI research and technology."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"that gap\" in paragraph 7 refers to _______.", options: ["the divide between application tools and foundational research", "the difference in funding between university programs and private startups", "the shortage of instructors qualified to teach AI courses", "the difference between domestic and foreign investment levels in Vietnam's AI sector"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what has attracted foreign technology firms to open research units in Vietnam?", options: ["Comparatively lower operating costs and a young, technically inclined workforce.", "Access to a domestic market of consumers willing to pay premium prices for AI products.", "Government subsidies covering the full cost of new data centers.", "Vietnam's advanced position in producing original AI research."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a factor that will influence whether Vietnam closes the gap between application-layer AI and foundational AI research?", options: ["Whether regulatory frameworks keep pace with rapid deployment.", "Whether venture funding continues over the long term rather than fading after initial enthusiasm.", "Whether the government reduces corporate tax rates for foreign AI companies.", "How effectively the education pipeline can be scaled."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, in which areas have domestic Vietnamese AI startups attracted notable venture funding?", options: ["Language processing for Vietnamese and software tools for small and medium-sized businesses.", "Government-funded infrastructure projects for national data centers.", "Large-scale chip manufacturing for international technology companies.", "Foundational research aimed at building original AI models from scratch."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the sustainability of Vietnam's current AI growth, based on the passage?", options: ["Vietnam's AI growth will certainly continue at its current pace regardless of any changes to research investment.", "Its continued growth may depend more on deeper investment in foundational research than on the pace of current application-layer development.", "Vietnamese universities have expanded AI-related coursework and launched new degree programs.", "Foreign firms opened research units in Vietnam because domestic startups had already achieved major breakthroughs in foundational AI research."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"trajectory\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a sudden and permanent stop to progress", "a legal document outlining a business agreement", "a physical location where research takes place", "the general path or direction something follows over time"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's purpose in paragraph 6?", options: ["To argue that foreign investment in Vietnam's AI sector should be banned.", "To offer a cautious critique that tempers earlier optimism.", "To criticize Vietnamese universities for failing to train enough AI specialists.", "To declare that Vietnam's AI development has completely failed to produce any meaningful progress."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "From Fringe to Mainstream: The Uncertain Science Behind Organic Food", paragraphs: ["Organic food occupies a strange position in the modern grocery aisle: still marketed, in many ways, as an alternative to \"conventional\" agriculture, even as it has grown into a multi-billion-dollar segment of that same mainstream food industry, sold in the very supermarket chains it was once defined against.", "What actually qualifies a product as \"organic\" is less universal than shoppers often assume. Regulatory bodies in different countries maintain their own certification standards, and although most schemes share a broad emphasis on avoiding synthetic pesticides, fertilizers, and genetically modified organisms, the specific rules — permitted exceptions, inspection frequency, allowable input substances — vary enough that a product certified organic in one jurisdiction would not necessarily qualify under another's standard. Even the length of the transition period a farm must complete before its produce can carry the label differs from one certification body to the next, adding yet another layer of inconsistency to what shoppers often assume is a single, universal standard.", "It is this patchwork of standards, more than any single scientific consensus, that shaped how the modern organic sector developed. The movement's roots trace back to early twentieth-century agricultural reformers who objected to the growing use of synthetic fertilizers on both philosophical and practical grounds, but organic food remained a fringe interest for decades, confined largely to small farms and specialty shops, until formal certification systems emerged toward the end of the century and gave retailers a standardized label they could market at scale. That single development, more than any breakthrough in agricultural science, is generally credited with turning organic food from a niche philosophy into a recognizable commercial category.", "That labeling shift, once in place, coincided with — and arguably helped drive — a period of rapid market growth, as organic products moved from dedicated health-food stores into ordinary supermarkets. Consumers who choose organic products cite a range of motivations, including perceived health benefits, environmental concerns, and animal welfare standards, though survey data suggests these motivations are not always well informed by what the certification itself actually guarantees, since an organic label speaks primarily to production methods rather than to nutritional content or environmental outcome directly. Retailers, for their part, have generally had little incentive to correct this misunderstanding, since a premium-priced product perceived as healthier and more environmentally responsible sells more readily than one accurately described as simply produced under a different set of farming rules.", "Whether organic food is meaningfully more nutritious than conventionally grown alternatives remains a contested question. Several large-scale reviews of the nutritional literature have found modestly higher levels of certain compounds, such as some antioxidants, in organic produce, but researchers conducting these reviews caution that the differences are often small, inconsistent across studies, and do not necessarily translate into any measurable difference in health outcomes for someone eating an otherwise ordinary diet. Where the evidence is somewhat firmer is in measures of pesticide residue, which tends to be consistently lower in organic produce, though researchers note that residue levels in conventionally grown food generally remain well within established safety limits regardless.", "The environmental case is similarly complicated, though for different reasons. Organic farming methods generally reduce the use of synthetic chemical inputs and can improve certain measures of soil health, but they also tend to produce lower yields per unit of land than conventional methods do. Critics of a wholesale shift toward organic production argue that, because more land would then be needed to produce the same amount of food, any localized environmental benefit could be offset by the broader ecological cost of converting additional land to agricultural use elsewhere. Proponents of organic methods counter that yield comparisons vary considerably by crop and region.", "None of this necessarily means the price premium consumers pay for organic products is unjustified, since that premium may reflect labor costs, certification fees, or lower yields as much as it reflects any verified nutritional or environmental advantage — but it does suggest that the marketing language surrounding organic food has, in places, outpaced what the underlying research can currently confirm, leaving consumers to weigh a genuinely uncertain body of evidence against a label that often implies more certainty than the science actually supports."], questions: [{ q: "According to the passage, what does an organic certification label primarily indicate?", options: ["The number of inspections a farm undergoes each year.", "The specific environmental impact of growing that particular batch of food.", "The exact nutritional value of the food compared with conventional alternatives.", "The farming methods used rather than nutritional or environmental results."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"patchwork\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["an inconsistent mixture of different", "a type of decorative fabric quilt", "a temporary emergency measure", "a single unified rule applied everywhere"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Scientific studies have consistently proven organic food to be both more nutritious and more environmentally friendly than conventional food.", "Nearly all consumers who buy organic food fully understand exactly what the certification does and does not guarantee.", "Organic certification standards vary considerably from one country's regulatory body to another.", "The organic industry expanded through standardized labels despite mixed scientific support."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"this\" at the beginning of paragraph 7 refers to _______.", options: ["the decision by early reformers to object to synthetic fertilizers", "the mixed scientific evidence on health and environmental benefits", "the variation in certification standards across different countries", "the growth of organic products from specialty stores into mainstream supermarkets"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why did organic food remain a \"fringe interest\" for decades?", options: ["Because standardized certification systems that could support large-scale retail marketing had not yet emerged.", "Because government regulations banned the sale of organic products until recently.", "Because conventional farming methods produced significantly higher-quality food at the time.", "Because early agricultural reformers lacked any philosophical objection to synthetic fertilizers."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason consumers choose organic products?", options: ["Environmental concerns.", "Perceived health benefits.", "An assumption among some buyers that the organic label directly guarantees higher nutritional content.", "A desire to support small, family-owned farms specifically."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what trade-off do critics raise regarding a large-scale shift to organic farming?", options: ["Organic farming requires significantly more synthetic chemical inputs than conventional methods.", "Organic certification fees are too expensive for small farms to afford.", "Lower yields require converting more land, offsetting local eco-benefits.", "Consumers are unwilling to pay the price premium organic food currently commands."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What does the passage suggest about the relationship between the organic price premium and its scientifically verified benefits?", options: ["Organic farming methods generally reduce the use of synthetic chemical inputs compared with conventional methods.", "Because organic food carries a price premium, researchers have concluded it must offer superior environmental benefits.", "The premium is entirely explained by the proven nutritional superiority of organic food over conventional alternatives.", "The premium may reflect production costs rather than proven scientific perks."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"offset\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["delayed until a later date by", "financially compensated for with a cash payment by", "counterbalanced or canceled out by", "completely eliminated by"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's purpose in discussing both the nutritional and environmental research?", options: ["To prove that conventional farming methods are scientifically superior to organic methods in every respect.", "To show that organic benefits are more uncertain than marketing implies.", "To argue that organic food provides no benefits whatsoever compared with conventional food.", "To criticize consumers for choosing to buy organic products."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Great Smog of 1952: A Disaster Measured in Disputed Numbers", paragraphs: ["Not only did visibility in London drop to near zero in some neighborhoods during the first week of December 1952, but the fog that caused it would go on to kill far more people than anyone at the time realized, making what became known as the Great Smog one of the deadliest environmental disasters in modern British history without initially being recognized as one at all.", "The immediate cause was meteorological: an unusually still, cold high-pressure system settled over the city and created what is known as a temperature inversion, in which a layer of warm air trapped a layer of colder air — and the pollutants suspended within it — close to the ground, preventing the usual vertical mixing that would otherwise have dispersed the smoke. Compounding this, postwar fuel shortages had pushed many households toward burning cheaper, lower-grade coal with a higher sulphur content, so that the smoke pouring from tens of thousands of chimneys that week was unusually rich in sulphur dioxide and fine particulate matter. Industrial emissions and diesel exhaust from the city's buses and trains added further pollutants to a mixture that, under normal weather conditions, would have dispersed within hours rather than lingering for days.", "For five days, the resulting smog brought much of the city to a standstill. Buses required a person walking ahead with a lantern to guide the driver; flights were grounded; indoor events, including a concert at Sadler's Wells, were reportedly cancelled because the smog had begun seeping into the auditorium itself. What was not immediately apparent, however, was the toll the smog was taking on respiratory health, since many of the deaths it caused were recorded, at the time, simply as ordinary cases of bronchitis or pneumonia rather than being connected to the fog outside.", "It is this misattribution that complicates any confident death toll for the event. Official figures compiled in the immediate aftermath put the number at around four thousand deaths, a figure based largely on comparing the week's mortality statistics with those of a typical December. Later epidemiological studies, applying more sophisticated statistical methods to reconstruct excess mortality over a longer window following the smog, have suggested considerably higher totals, with some researchers estimating that the true figure may have exceeded twelve thousand once delayed deaths from prolonged respiratory damage are included — though how far to extend that window, and how to separate smog-related deaths from other seasonal causes, remains a matter of genuine methodological disagreement rather than a settled question.", "What is less disputed is the policy response the disaster eventually provoked. Public and political pressure, though slower to build than the scale of the event might suggest, contributed to the passage of the Clean Air Act in 1956, which established designated \"smoke control areas\" in which only smokeless fuels could be burned and gave local authorities new powers to regulate industrial emissions. Implementation, however, was gradual rather than immediate, with many households continuing to burn coal for years afterward as smokeless alternatives remained more expensive and less widely available.", "Some historians caution against treating the Clean Air Act as a simple, direct response to the Great Smog alone. The broader shift away from coal — driven by the increasing availability of gas, electricity, and oil for domestic heating — was already underway before 1952, they note, which makes it difficult to isolate how much of the resulting improvement in air quality can be attributed specifically to the legislation rather than to economic trends that would likely have continued regardless. This does not mean the legislation was irrelevant, these historians add, only that crediting it as the singular turning point risks oversimplifying a change that several overlapping forces were already pushing in the same direction.", "This same uncertainty , ironically, runs through how the Great Smog itself is remembered. The unresolved question of exactly how many people died has been invoked both to underscore the disaster's severity — since even the lower official estimate was substantial — and, by more cautious historians, as a reminder not to treat any single figure as definitive simply because it has been repeated often enough to feel authoritative. Whether the event's long-term political impact would have been meaningfully different had the lower, contemporaneous estimate never been revised upward is a question that, like the precise death toll itself, is unlikely ever to be settled with confidence."], questions: [{ q: "According to the passage, what combined with the temperature inversion to make the 1952 smog especially dangerous?", options: ["Households burning lower-grade coal with higher sulphur content due to postwar fuel shortages.", "Buses being unable to operate without a person walking ahead with a lantern.", "A shortage of coal that forced most households to stop heating their homes entirely.", "A sudden increase in vehicle traffic across the city that week."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"invoked\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["cited or referred to in support of an argument", "financially compensated for", "legally prohibited from being discussed", "physically summoned to appear in person"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The Clean Air Act of 1956 was clearly the sole cause of Britain's long-term improvement in air quality, independent of any other economic trends.", "Historians now universally agree on the exact number of deaths caused by the Great Smog.", "The 1952 Great Smog caused major unrecognized casualties, leading to policy debates over its exact long-term impact.", "A temperature inversion trapped smoke and pollutants close to the ground over London for five days in December 1952."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"This same uncertainty\" at the beginning of paragraph 7 refers to _______.", options: ["the decision by Parliament to pass the Clean Air Act in 1956", "the five-day period during which the smog reduced visibility across London", "the challenge of measuring exact figures and causes for both casualties and legislative impact", "the disagreement among historians about when gas and electricity became widely available"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why were many smog-related deaths not initially recognized as such?", options: ["They were classified as routine cases of bronchitis or pneumonia instead of smog-related illnesses.", "Most affected residents had already left the city before the smog worsened.", "Hospitals refused to treat patients during the five days of the smog.", "Government officials deliberately hid the true death toll from the public."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason the exact death toll of the Great Smog remains disputed?", options: ["Uncertainty about how far to extend the time window used to count delayed deaths.", "Disagreement over how to separate smog-related deaths from other seasonal causes.", "The fact that many deaths were originally recorded under ordinary respiratory diagnoses rather than linked to the smog.", "Missing medical records from local regional hospitals during the winter months."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the Clean Air Act of 1956 specifically establish?", options: ["A nationwide ban on all coal burning starting immediately in 1956.", "Mandatory lantern-guided bus routes during periods of low visibility.", "Designated \"smoke control areas\" where only smokeless fuels could be burned.", "A fixed compensation fund for families of smog victims."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What does the passage suggest about the relationship between the Great Smog and the Clean Air Act of 1956?", options: ["The temperature inversion prevented the usual vertical mixing that would have dispersed the smoke.", "The Act was passed immediately after the smog and single-handedly caused all subsequent improvements in British air quality.", "The Act was spurred by the event, but shifting energy trends complicate its sole impact.", "Because gas and electricity became more widely available, the government was pressured into causing the Great Smog to occur."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"isolate\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to completely eliminate any possible cause", "to separate out and identify one specific cause among several", "to financially insure against a future risk", "to physically quarantine a sick person"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's purpose in paragraph 6?", options: ["To argue that the Clean Air Act had no effect on air quality whatsoever.", "To challenge the simplistic view that the Clean Air Act single-handedly solved air pollution.", "To criticize historians for failing to study the Great Smog in enough detail.", "To prove definitively that economic trends, not legislation, caused all improvements in air quality."], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
];
